WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.134 --> 00:00:03.550
And now it gives
me great pleasure

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:07.780
to welcome the 17th President
of MIT, Rafael Reif, who

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:12.204
will speak to us first, and then
introduce our keynote speaker.

00:00:12.204 --> 00:00:16.100
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:25.410
[INAUDIBLE], let me
see if it works for me.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:26.435
Good morning.

00:00:26.435 --> 00:00:27.620
Good morning!

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:30.200
That was awesome!

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:33.130
Thank you, Lulu, for
your nice introduction.

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:37.730
And many thanks to everyone
who helped to bring us together

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:41.300
this morning, including our
host, the Committee on Race

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:45.670
and Diversity, the MLK Planning
Committee, and our Institute

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:49.740
Community and Equity
Officer, Ed Bertschinger.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:53.410
And above all, a huge thank
you for our student speakers,

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:56.870
our incredible student
speakers, Itoro and Sergio.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:59.040
You make us all so proud.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:00.016
Thank you.

00:01:00.016 --> 00:01:03.824
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:11.070
This morning, this
morning we come together

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:15.150
to celebrate in the
spirit of Dr. King.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:19.740
The value of activism,
especially student activism,

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:22.530
in driving social change.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:26.650
So I begin by congratulating all
the winners of this year's MLK

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:28.860
Leadership Award.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:31.250
I recognize that the
staff and faculty

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:36.550
who work on these issues play a
profound role in our community.

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:38.900
But in the context
of today's event,

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:42.480
I offer special recognition
to the student winners,

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.770
Rasheed Auguste of the
Black Student Union,

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:49.160
the Black Graduate Student
Association, George Chao,

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:55.670
an activist for graduate student
concerns, and Fossil Free MIT.

00:01:55.670 --> 00:02:00.080
In terms of student
activism on our campus,

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:05.410
this has been quite a year--
an extraordinary year.

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:09.810
On topics from race,
inclusion, and social justice,

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:13.300
to climate change, our
students have in many ways

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:17.430
become our teachers.

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:22.400
That has been true on
campuses across the country.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.980
But our students have
done things differently,

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.390
in a way that I
particularly admire.

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:34.145
I want to share just one
example, an image that I

00:02:34.145 --> 00:02:36.980
will never forget.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.765
I chair a group at MIT
called Academic Council--

00:02:39.765 --> 00:02:43.650
it was referred to earlier
this morning-- about 30

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:49.230
of MIT's top academic and
administrative leaders.

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:52.180
We meet Tuesday mornings.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:57.540
One Tuesday in January, for the
first time that I can remember,

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:01.872
a group of students were
invited and came to our meeting.

00:03:01.872 --> 00:03:05.640
The leaders of the
BSU and the BGSA

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:10.790
presented their ideas for making
the MIT community more diverse,

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:14.070
and more welcoming for all.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.840
Many of you know the students.

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:22.140
I'm telling you, they
were magnificent!

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:24.150
They were thoughtful.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:30.720
They were creative, persistent,
specific, collaborative,

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:34.300
constructive, and serious.

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:37.250
They went out of their
way to incorporate

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:41.120
the views of students from
other underrepresented groups

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:42.810
as well.

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:48.610
And with Academic Council, they
set the tone of mutual respect,

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:53.480
and they earned tremendous
respect in return.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:55.450
The students brought
their recommendations

00:03:55.450 --> 00:04:00.320
to address issues specific
to MIT today-- improving

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:04.890
orientation, and adding booster
shots of inclusion education

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:09.690
after freshman year;
enhancing financial aid;

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:13.350
collecting and publishing new
types of data about the student

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:17.910
experience; hiring more
diverse mental health staff;

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:21.360
providing training to
overcome unconscious bias.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:24.280
And the list goes on.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:27.650
Today, these students
are collaborating

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:30.680
with Academic Council's
brand-new working group

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:32.670
on inclusion.

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:35.830
Together, they are addressing
these recommendations

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:39.050
and the problems
they aim to solve.

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:42.470
The working group will share
a public progress report

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:44.710
in time for spring break.

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:49.220
And I'm confident
that we are on a path

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:54.240
to sustained and
meaningful change.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:58.270
You may find interesting to
know that Academic Council has

00:04:58.270 --> 00:05:02.090
volunteered to be the pilot
group for unconscious bias

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:04.430
training.

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:09.170
For this exceptional
example of student activism,

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:12.255
of initiative and
constructive leadership,

00:05:12.255 --> 00:05:19.216
I'd like to publicly say here
to them, thank you, and bravo.

00:05:19.216 --> 00:05:22.632
[APPLAUSE]

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:33.830
As one of our student
leaders put it recently,

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:37.620
"It is wonderful to
see the gears of MIT

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:43.810
go to work on a problem,"
and I could not agree more.

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:47.300
One last observation--
when these students

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:49.850
spoke to Academic
Council, they made

00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:52.580
it clear that some of
what needs to happen

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:56.740
has to do with human values.

00:05:56.740 --> 00:05:59.610
They are asking
us to say out loud

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:03.280
that we value the
diversity of our community.

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:05.410
I do.

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:08.110
They are urging all of
us at MIT to maintain

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:12.000
a human perspective,
and to remind each other

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.090
that while the quality of our
students' work is important,

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:20.670
their mental and physical
health is most important.

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:23.410
I agree.

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:27.150
And finally, they are
making a powerful case

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:29.970
that a more welcoming,
more inclusive

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:34.350
MIT would be better for
absolutely everyone.

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:36.710
They are right.

00:06:36.710 --> 00:06:38.490
And I look forward
to working with them

00:06:38.490 --> 00:06:42.830
to make this vision real.

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:47.080
And now I'm honored to introduce
this morning's keynote speaker.

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:49.940
Freeman Hrabowski is
President of the University

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:52.780
of Maryland, Baltimore County.

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:58.970
He has held that
job for 24 years.

00:06:58.970 --> 00:07:02.720
Now, I've been president here
for three and a half years!

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:05.290
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:07.810
I must tell you,
Freeman's record

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:11.750
will be tough, if not
impossible, to beat.

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:15.600
But I take comfort
in the knowledge

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:20.060
that he had a tremendous
head start over me.

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:22.615
He was born eight
days before me.

00:07:22.615 --> 00:07:27.416
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:27.416 --> 00:07:31.610
As many of you know, through
his leadership of UMBC,

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:36.020
Freeman has accomplished
something deeply important.

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:38.730
At MIT, some of the
work we admire most

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:42.290
comes at the intersection
of theory and practice--

00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:46.130
when a powerful idea translates
into effective solutions

00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:48.370
in the real world.

00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:52.100
President Hrabowski has
become the leading theorist,

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:54.860
and the most accomplished
practitioner,

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:58.850
of the science of empowering
students of color and women

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:03.590
to succeed at the highest
levels in STEM fields.

00:08:03.590 --> 00:08:08.000
UMBC's a predominantly
white institution.

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:13.990
By living up to his vision, it
has become a national leader

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:16.770
in the number of its
minority graduates

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:21.330
who go on to earn doctorates
in medicine, in the sciences,

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:23.200
and in engineering.

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:25.400
What's more, his
efforts have become

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:28.210
models for colleges
and universities

00:08:28.210 --> 00:08:30.500
across the country.

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:33.210
For example, in
1988, Freeman helped

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:38.250
launch UMBC's widely recognized
Meyerhoff Scholars Program,

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:42.429
for young black men interested
in science and engineering.

00:08:42.429 --> 00:08:46.480
The program provides each
scholar with academic support,

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:49.060
as well as personal
advising and mentoring,

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:52.870
year-round for the first
two years of college.

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:56.180
Their success rates
are through the roof.

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:58.010
And a very high
percentage of them

00:08:58.010 --> 00:09:02.221
go on to graduate
school in STEM fields.

00:09:02.221 --> 00:09:06.580
At MIT, we were so impressed,
that four years ago, we

00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:09.850
adapted key features
of the Meyerhoff model

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:14.360
for a program we
call Interphase EDGE.

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:18.070
It serves about 70 MIT
undergraduates a year,

00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:23.440
and the first Interphase Edge
cohort will graduate this June.

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:26.500
And we have Freeman to
thank for the model,

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:29.450
and for the inspiration.

00:09:29.450 --> 00:09:31.650
Freeman has also
co-authored three books

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:34.560
on how to help young
people succeed,

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:37.860
and he has won a
great many honors.

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:42.870
In 2009, TIME Magazine named
him one of America's Best

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:45.670
College Presidents.

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:49.740
Three years later, they declared
him one of 100 Most Influential

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:53.490
People in the world.

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:56.150
And in 2012,
President Obama asked

00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:59.580
him to chair the newly
created President's Advisory

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:02.460
Commission on Educational
Excellence for African

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:03.820
Americans.

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:07.030
Freeman's awards and
achievements are remarkable,

00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:10.140
but I'd like to
mention just one more.

00:10:10.140 --> 00:10:14.610
In 2015, US News &amp;
World Report named

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:20.500
UMBC one of the most innovative
national universities.

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:24.250
MIT made that list, too.

00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:29.960
In fact, our two institutions
came in third and fourth.

00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:31.630
Never mind which one was which!

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:40.780
But, number one
was Arizona State.

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:42.700
Number two was Stanford.

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:45.100
So I'm planning to strike
a deal with Freeman

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:47.450
for us to stick together,
and earn the two

00:10:47.450 --> 00:10:48.860
top spots next time!

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:50.330
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:54.250
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:01.850
In short, as our
diverse community

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:04.960
seeks a path that
combines the highest

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:07.710
level of intellectual
achievement

00:11:07.710 --> 00:11:11.680
with a warm sense of welcome
and inclusion for all,

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:15.610
we could not ask for a
better guide and friend

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:17.070
than this morning's speaker.

00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:19.840
Please welcome President
Freeman Hrabowski.

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:24.340
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:36.720
I enjoyed hearing that
we are the same age,

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:40.590
and that you were a number
three and we were number four.

00:11:40.590 --> 00:11:46.420
You had about 100 years ahead
of us, you were founded in 1861.

00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:48.540
And you have quite a bit
more in your endowment.

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:52.190
So I think pound for pound,
we're doing OK, also,

00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:52.885
all right?

00:11:52.885 --> 00:11:56.322
You get that, all right?

00:11:56.322 --> 00:12:01.160
[APPLAUSE]

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:03.390
William Carlos
Williams once said

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:07.690
that it's difficult to
find news in portrait.

00:12:07.690 --> 00:12:09.930
And yet men die
miserably every day,

00:12:09.930 --> 00:12:11.810
because of a lack of
what is found there.

00:12:11.810 --> 00:12:14.240
And so I begin with the
words of our beloved,

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:17.130
and now late, Maya
Angelou, who looked

00:12:17.130 --> 00:12:21.170
into the eyes of this
country and said,

00:12:21.170 --> 00:12:25.970
"Lift up your eyes upon
this day, breaking for you.

00:12:25.970 --> 00:12:29.470
Give birth again to the dream.

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:34.990
Women, children, men--
take it, this dream,

00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:38.270
into the palms of
your hands, molded

00:12:38.270 --> 00:12:41.120
into the image of
your most public self,

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:46.270
sculpted into the shape
of your most private need.

00:12:46.270 --> 00:12:48.940
Here on the pulse
of this new day,

00:12:48.940 --> 00:12:52.140
you may have the grace
to look up and out

00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:56.300
and into your sister's eyes,
and into your brother's face,

00:12:56.300 --> 00:13:00.780
and say simply, very simply,
with hope, good morning."

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:02.129
Good morning to MIT.

00:13:02.129 --> 00:13:02.670
Good morning.

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:04.260
Give her a hand for poetry!

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:05.650
Give her a hand for poetry!

00:13:05.650 --> 00:13:11.780
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:11.780 --> 00:13:15.460
I was so impressed by
the students today,

00:13:15.460 --> 00:13:18.300
I got goosebumps.

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:21.300
And your theme is
truth and power--

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:24.480
I would have called
it truth to power,

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:28.580
as they both spoke so eloquently
about the challenges we face

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:32.110
in our society, the challenges
we face on our campuses,

00:13:32.110 --> 00:13:34.290
whether it's MIT or UMBC.

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:37.810
The fact that we can
be better than we are,

00:13:37.810 --> 00:13:42.220
and that students have
a very important role

00:13:42.220 --> 00:13:46.980
to play in helping us look
in the mirror as a nation,

00:13:46.980 --> 00:13:49.060
and a society.

00:13:49.060 --> 00:13:52.690
As institutions give all
the students in the room

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:54.486
a round of applause.

00:13:54.486 --> 00:13:58.968
[APPLAUSE]

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:10.240
I was delighted to talk with
Rafael-- as a fellow president,

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:12.800
as somebody who cares
deeply about these issues,

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:15.380
and who was asking me
really wonderful questions

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:17.850
about my perceptions, about
my students who've come here.

00:14:17.850 --> 00:14:19.590
I've got several
students here today--

00:14:19.590 --> 00:14:21.510
if I have an alum,
who's here today,

00:14:21.510 --> 00:14:24.004
who's here at MIT, or
somewhere at Harvard MIT,

00:14:24.004 --> 00:14:25.170
there's several of you here.

00:14:25.170 --> 00:14:25.610
Are you in the room?

00:14:25.610 --> 00:14:26.430
Stand up if you are.

00:14:26.430 --> 00:14:28.150
If you're in the room, stand
up and give them a round

00:14:28.150 --> 00:14:29.500
of applause, wherever they are.

00:14:29.500 --> 00:14:30.985
There's some of you here.

00:14:30.985 --> 00:14:34.945
[APPLAUSE]

00:14:34.945 --> 00:14:36.430
Very nice.

00:14:36.430 --> 00:14:39.400
Very nice.

00:14:39.400 --> 00:14:41.590
Very proud of them,
very proud of them,

00:14:41.590 --> 00:14:43.840
getting rid of the finished
PhDs in the sciences,

00:14:43.840 --> 00:14:45.889
and MD PhDs between
Harvard and the MIT.

00:14:45.889 --> 00:14:47.930
And we've had a number of
students that come here

00:14:47.930 --> 00:14:48.680
and to do well.

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:51.420
I dedicate this
talk to two people.

00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:55.860
One is Robert Meyerhoff, who's
an actually a graduate of MIT,

00:14:55.860 --> 00:15:02.180
all the way back to the '40s,
and a wonderful philanthropist

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:06.440
who worked with us from the
beginning of our program

00:15:06.440 --> 00:15:08.315
to think about how
do we help more--

00:15:08.315 --> 00:15:10.920
at the time, African
Americans in particular,

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:12.060
to succeed in science.

00:15:12.060 --> 00:15:14.940
On a campus predominantly
white, when I first got there,

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:17.820
where students of
color, and particularly

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:21.180
there in that Baltimore
area, black kids

00:15:21.180 --> 00:15:23.430
were not doing well in science.

00:15:23.430 --> 00:15:26.210
And we started with young
black males at a time

00:15:26.210 --> 00:15:29.990
when nobody wanted to
talk about males of color.

00:15:29.990 --> 00:15:31.780
And it was Bob
Meyerhoff who said,

00:15:31.780 --> 00:15:34.020
I'm already working on
some issues with my wife's

00:15:34.020 --> 00:15:36.760
alma mater [INAUDIBLE] on women,
and I want to see-- he said,

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:40.330
because everything I see
on TV about black males,

00:15:40.330 --> 00:15:43.050
if it's not sports, it's
about handcuffs, or guns.

00:15:43.050 --> 00:15:44.900
And this was back
in the late '80s.

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:47.680
Give him a round of applause
for having that kind of vision.

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:52.850
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:52.850 --> 00:15:56.390
And now we are proud to say
we have students of all races.

00:15:56.390 --> 00:15:58.800
It's still heavily African
American and Hispanic,

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:00.600
but males and females.

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:02.200
And we lead the
country, not only

00:16:02.200 --> 00:16:04.950
in reducing blacks who go
on to get PhDs in STEM,

00:16:04.950 --> 00:16:06.370
among predominantly
white schools,

00:16:06.370 --> 00:16:09.060
but for any type of
institution, we're

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:12.065
number one in producing blacks
are going to get M.D. PhDs.

00:16:12.065 --> 00:16:13.773
Give us a big round
of applause for that.

00:16:13.773 --> 00:16:19.090
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:19.090 --> 00:16:21.250
And the other person
I want to mention

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:23.730
is another graduate
of your institution

00:16:23.730 --> 00:16:26.950
at the undergrad
level, Janice Lumpkin.

00:16:26.950 --> 00:16:29.130
The late Janice Lumpkin,
and it's hard for me

00:16:29.130 --> 00:16:32.230
to talk about her, because
she graduated from here

00:16:32.230 --> 00:16:33.980
in chemical engineering.

00:16:33.980 --> 00:16:36.510
And she went to the
University of Pennsylvania,

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:38.680
became the second
African American woman

00:16:38.680 --> 00:16:41.380
to get a PhD in
chemical engineering.

00:16:41.380 --> 00:16:46.560
And she came to our campus,
and she was amazing.

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:51.320
And she helped us to develop
at my program in those years,

00:16:51.320 --> 00:16:52.240
and supported it.

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:55.050
And it was the first time,
so many of my students

00:16:55.050 --> 00:16:57.360
whether-- and I have
students from 150 countries--

00:16:57.360 --> 00:16:58.990
it was the first
time most of them

00:16:58.990 --> 00:17:02.450
had ever seen an African
American woman faculty member

00:17:02.450 --> 00:17:04.190
in engineering of any kind.

00:17:04.190 --> 00:17:05.170
You get my point?

00:17:05.170 --> 00:17:08.410
So she began with
that ability to say,

00:17:08.410 --> 00:17:10.720
it is possible to do it.

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:15.839
But then she brought to it
such compassion and brilliance,

00:17:15.839 --> 00:17:19.160
and this ability to
pull people together,

00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:21.940
and to say, we are better
than this, we can do this.

00:17:21.940 --> 00:17:24.829
And she inspired so many of
the Meyerhoffs, but so many

00:17:24.829 --> 00:17:27.200
of my white students to go
on to graduate students--

00:17:27.200 --> 00:17:28.369
and particularly women.

00:17:28.369 --> 00:17:31.800
She tragically
died in childbirth.

00:17:31.800 --> 00:17:37.150
But she had just gotten tenure,
and we celebrate her legacy.

00:17:37.150 --> 00:17:40.320
And I want you today,
to think about the idea

00:17:40.320 --> 00:17:44.580
that this institution
produced both above Meyerhoff,

00:17:44.580 --> 00:17:47.076
a wonderful philanthropist.

00:17:47.076 --> 00:17:48.825
People say, well, was
Bob Meyerhoff black?

00:17:48.825 --> 00:17:50.960
I said, black, his
name is Meyerhoff!

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:52.950
They said, well, your
name is Hrabowski!

00:17:55.960 --> 00:17:58.450
A wonderful Jewish
philanthropist

00:17:58.450 --> 00:18:02.200
who simply cared about people,
and other people's children,

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:04.740
and then a wonderful,
brilliant young woman

00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:07.940
from Shaker Heights,
Ohio, from Cleveland,

00:18:07.940 --> 00:18:11.930
who came here and had some
people who cared about her,

00:18:11.930 --> 00:18:16.890
had some challenges, yes, but
who went on and got that PhD

00:18:16.890 --> 00:18:22.690
and who knew the significance
of giving back to others.

00:18:22.690 --> 00:18:26.720
And so, I want you to think
with me for the next few minutes

00:18:26.720 --> 00:18:31.980
about the story of America,
about your story-- students,

00:18:31.980 --> 00:18:37.210
faculty, staff members alumni--
each of us has a story.

00:18:37.210 --> 00:18:41.370
If I were to give you
a theory, in this room,

00:18:41.370 --> 00:18:42.370
you might be interested.

00:18:42.370 --> 00:18:45.550
Most audiences would
not be interested.

00:18:45.550 --> 00:18:47.300
In this room, you
might be interested.

00:18:47.300 --> 00:18:49.960
And I will tell you that
if you look at my writings,

00:18:49.960 --> 00:18:52.380
you'll see a theory of
social transformation,

00:18:52.380 --> 00:18:55.160
saying that our
universities, predominately

00:18:55.160 --> 00:18:58.470
white universities-- I wrote
it several years ago-- must

00:18:58.470 --> 00:19:03.520
look in the mirror and rethink
the culture of the institution.

00:19:03.520 --> 00:19:06.560
That is not just about
students changing.

00:19:06.560 --> 00:19:08.420
I saw this on my
own campus, I still

00:19:08.420 --> 00:19:10.250
see it as we work
on these challenges.

00:19:10.250 --> 00:19:13.420
It is about the entire
institution changing, thinking

00:19:13.420 --> 00:19:16.220
about its values, its
assumptions, the way it treats

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:18.340
people, empowering people.

00:19:18.340 --> 00:19:21.710
That would be the
theory I would give you.

00:19:21.710 --> 00:19:23.570
And we used it in
our-- I chaired--

00:19:23.570 --> 00:19:25.190
I was the PI of
our advance program

00:19:25.190 --> 00:19:27.510
when we talked about more
women faculty members.

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:29.280
We've used it with the
Meyerhoff scholars,

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:32.210
with our Center for Women
in IT, but more important,

00:19:32.210 --> 00:19:36.230
for the entire institution
to say, all of us

00:19:36.230 --> 00:19:38.150
must be involved in this work.

00:19:38.150 --> 00:19:41.800
If we are to pull people in
who've not been historically

00:19:41.800 --> 00:19:46.530
represented at the institution,
we must hear their voices,

00:19:46.530 --> 00:19:49.860
we must think about who we
are, and who we want to become.

00:19:49.860 --> 00:19:54.110
And my central point today,
as I tell you my own story,

00:19:54.110 --> 00:19:59.060
is that the way we think
about ourselves, as a society,

00:19:59.060 --> 00:20:04.200
as the Academy, as MIT, as
UMBC-- the language that we

00:20:04.200 --> 00:20:07.650
use, the way we interact
with each other, the values

00:20:07.650 --> 00:20:11.210
that we hold will shape
who we are not only today,

00:20:11.210 --> 00:20:12.080
but in the future.

00:20:12.080 --> 00:20:16.210
In other words, our
dreams and our values

00:20:16.210 --> 00:20:19.050
really become who we are.

00:20:19.050 --> 00:20:22.650
And so, I'm sitting in the
back of church, in the middle

00:20:22.650 --> 00:20:25.520
of the week as a 12-year-old.

00:20:25.520 --> 00:20:30.520
And I was always a little
nerdy kid, loving math.

00:20:30.520 --> 00:20:31.540
I've always loved math.

00:20:31.540 --> 00:20:34.460
I get goosebumps
still, doing math.

00:20:34.460 --> 00:20:38.630
And I'm forced to be there,
because my parents say,

00:20:38.630 --> 00:20:40.869
we want you to
hear this guy talk.

00:20:40.869 --> 00:20:42.410
And they bribe me
with the two things

00:20:42.410 --> 00:20:46.670
I love most-- eating, and math.

00:20:46.670 --> 00:20:50.900
So I'm eating M&amp;Ms-- the
good kind, with the peanuts,

00:20:50.900 --> 00:20:51.695
and doing my math.

00:20:51.695 --> 00:20:54.454
Now, I'm 12, but I'm about
to go to the 10th grade.

00:20:54.454 --> 00:20:56.370
I've skipped grades, not
because I'm so smart,

00:20:56.370 --> 00:20:59.570
but because I had wonderfully
educated parents who

00:20:59.570 --> 00:21:01.240
had been working
with word problems

00:21:01.240 --> 00:21:04.080
all my life, who had taught
me to study Dostoevsky when

00:21:04.080 --> 00:21:06.710
I was in middle school, because
they knew the world wouldn't be

00:21:06.710 --> 00:21:08.970
fair to a little colored child.

00:21:08.970 --> 00:21:10.580
And that I had to
be twice as good,

00:21:10.580 --> 00:21:13.060
and had to read broadly
and study a lot.

00:21:13.060 --> 00:21:14.829
And my mama, who taught
math and English,

00:21:14.829 --> 00:21:16.370
would work with my
father, and they'd

00:21:16.370 --> 00:21:18.950
work with me to make
me ready, so that one

00:21:18.950 --> 00:21:22.600
day when I had a chance to be in
class with white kids, one day,

00:21:22.600 --> 00:21:25.152
I would not feel inferior.

00:21:25.152 --> 00:21:26.860
And so I'm sitting in
the back of church,

00:21:26.860 --> 00:21:29.410
and I'm getting fatter
and smarter all the time

00:21:29.410 --> 00:21:31.590
as I'm doing this math.

00:21:31.590 --> 00:21:34.070
You know, the Southerners--
Atlanta-- we like those cheeks.

00:21:34.070 --> 00:21:34.970
You know that.

00:21:34.970 --> 00:21:37.080
Got to have a little
padding, right?

00:21:37.080 --> 00:21:39.210
And the guy says,
if the children

00:21:39.210 --> 00:21:42.730
participate in this peaceful
protest, all of America

00:21:42.730 --> 00:21:45.580
will understand that even
our babies, our children,

00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:48.552
know the difference
between right and wrong.

00:21:48.552 --> 00:21:50.010
And I look up,
because I'm thinking

00:21:50.010 --> 00:21:51.593
about these hand-me-down
books that we

00:21:51.593 --> 00:21:54.480
have to use our schools,
in our all-black schools.

00:21:54.480 --> 00:21:56.870
And my parents are not
allowed to buy my books,

00:21:56.870 --> 00:21:58.890
because then I'd be
different from other kids.

00:21:58.890 --> 00:22:01.896
So you have to use
these raggedy books that

00:22:01.896 --> 00:22:04.270
came from the white schools
when they finished using them

00:22:04.270 --> 00:22:05.030
after years.

00:22:05.030 --> 00:22:07.830
Awful for us, to
have to feel that we

00:22:07.830 --> 00:22:12.440
were so second-class, that we
got their hand-me-down books.

00:22:12.440 --> 00:22:15.180
And so I look up, and I
said, who is that guy?

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:19.390
And of course, his name
was Dr. Martin Luther King.

00:22:19.390 --> 00:22:22.520
And I was absolutely
transformed,

00:22:22.520 --> 00:22:27.760
because his message was this--
that the world of tomorrow

00:22:27.760 --> 00:22:31.420
could be better than
the world of today.

00:22:31.420 --> 00:22:35.190
And that I, a child,
could be empowered

00:22:35.190 --> 00:22:37.449
to be a part of
that transformation.

00:22:37.449 --> 00:22:39.240
That it wasn't just
about what he would do,

00:22:39.240 --> 00:22:41.870
or what my parents would do--
he wanted me to be involved.

00:22:41.870 --> 00:22:43.960
I go home, and I
say, I've got to go.

00:22:43.960 --> 00:22:46.190
I've got to march in
this peaceful protest.

00:22:46.190 --> 00:22:49.570
And my parents look at me
and say, absolutely not.

00:22:49.570 --> 00:22:50.390
No, you cannot.

00:22:50.390 --> 00:22:53.370
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:53.370 --> 00:22:57.440
And I am so-- not upset,
not academically perturbed.

00:22:57.440 --> 00:22:58.740
I am pissed, all right?

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:00.800
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:00.800 --> 00:23:01.579
I am so upset.

00:23:01.579 --> 00:23:04.120
And I look at them and I said,
you know, you guys made me go,

00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:04.995
I did not want to go.

00:23:04.995 --> 00:23:08.502
You told me to listen,
I actually did listen.

00:23:08.502 --> 00:23:09.960
He tells me what
he wants me to do,

00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:11.490
and now you tell
me I can't do it?

00:23:11.490 --> 00:23:13.840
You are hypocrites!

00:23:13.840 --> 00:23:17.230
At that time--
students, you did not

00:23:17.230 --> 00:23:19.520
tell your parents they
were hypocrites, right?

00:23:19.520 --> 00:23:20.750
Go to your room, boy!

00:23:20.750 --> 00:23:23.240
And for that night, I
knew I was in trouble.

00:23:23.240 --> 00:23:24.870
But the next morning,
they came in.

00:23:24.870 --> 00:23:26.590
It was the first
time I'd ever seen

00:23:26.590 --> 00:23:31.390
my parents looking-- you could
tell they had been crying.

00:23:31.390 --> 00:23:35.520
And they said, it wasn't
that we did not trust you.

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:38.870
We know that if you go,
you will go to jail,

00:23:38.870 --> 00:23:44.810
and we don't trust the people
in jail who will be over you.

00:23:44.810 --> 00:23:48.030
But if you are
determined to do this,

00:23:48.030 --> 00:23:49.610
and you've thought
about it, and they

00:23:49.610 --> 00:23:50.850
said, why do you want to go?

00:23:50.850 --> 00:23:55.972
And I started, I said,
these hand-me-down books.

00:23:55.972 --> 00:23:57.930
I've got some good
teachers, but everybody says

00:23:57.930 --> 00:23:59.307
the white schools are better.

00:23:59.307 --> 00:24:00.890
And you tell me you
want me to get in.

00:24:00.890 --> 00:24:02.973
We go through this argument,
and we talk about it.

00:24:02.973 --> 00:24:07.310
And then they finally say, we
will put you in God's hands.

00:24:07.310 --> 00:24:09.830
Now, if the situation
were reversed,

00:24:09.830 --> 00:24:12.030
and my kid, who is
now grown, at 12--

00:24:12.030 --> 00:24:13.940
I'm not sure I would
have allowed him to go.

00:24:13.940 --> 00:24:15.020
You get my point?

00:24:15.020 --> 00:24:16.130
It's scary!

00:24:16.130 --> 00:24:19.440
And I was not a
courageous kid, students!

00:24:19.440 --> 00:24:23.110
The only thing I'd ever attacked
in my life was a math problem.

00:24:23.110 --> 00:24:24.010
All right?

00:24:24.010 --> 00:24:25.660
If a fight broke
out, I was running

00:24:25.660 --> 00:24:27.270
the other way, all right?

00:24:27.270 --> 00:24:28.020
OK?

00:24:28.020 --> 00:24:29.580
Not even ashamed to admit it.

00:24:29.580 --> 00:24:30.590
But here's the point.

00:24:30.590 --> 00:24:32.830
We did go, and I did
leave my little group,

00:24:32.830 --> 00:24:35.740
because they were kids as
young as eight and nine and 10,

00:24:35.740 --> 00:24:38.150
and while I was only 12,
I was a high school kid.

00:24:38.150 --> 00:24:39.770
I was more mature.

00:24:39.770 --> 00:24:42.660
And here's the point.

00:24:42.660 --> 00:24:48.370
We learned in that five days
in jail, horrible experiences,

00:24:48.370 --> 00:24:53.480
but we also learned that even
children can be empowered.

00:24:53.480 --> 00:24:55.140
To know they can
make a difference.

00:24:55.140 --> 00:24:57.460
And there is my message
to you, students.

00:24:57.460 --> 00:25:01.150
That yes, you do have the
right, and the ability

00:25:01.150 --> 00:25:03.830
to speak truth to power.

00:25:03.830 --> 00:25:07.712
Give yourselves a hand again
for speaking truth to power.

00:25:07.712 --> 00:25:13.520
[APPLAUSE]

00:25:13.520 --> 00:25:15.154
And so, what does
that mean today?

00:25:15.154 --> 00:25:16.320
What difference did it make?

00:25:16.320 --> 00:25:19.110
Well, interestingly
enough, I wrote an article,

00:25:19.110 --> 00:25:21.980
and it was so amazing
to me to realize

00:25:21.980 --> 00:25:26.140
that most of the people
in this country today

00:25:26.140 --> 00:25:29.670
were not born, Rafael, in 1963.

00:25:29.670 --> 00:25:30.200
Watch this.

00:25:30.200 --> 00:25:33.390
How many of you were not even
in the world in 1963, had not

00:25:33.390 --> 00:25:34.380
been born?

00:25:34.380 --> 00:25:35.507
That is disgusting.

00:25:35.507 --> 00:25:36.506
I want you to know that.

00:25:36.506 --> 00:25:37.865
[LAUGHTER]

00:25:37.865 --> 00:25:40.120
I want to tell you to
get out, just get out.

00:25:40.120 --> 00:25:42.800
[LAUGHTER]

00:25:42.800 --> 00:25:50.090
It turns out, 2/3 of Americans
were born after 1963.

00:25:50.090 --> 00:25:51.740
So the experience
that you hear me

00:25:51.740 --> 00:25:54.300
talking about for most of you,
is like something in a book.

00:25:54.300 --> 00:25:55.590
You getting my point.

00:25:55.590 --> 00:25:58.800
And I am that connection
with those of my generation,

00:25:58.800 --> 00:26:01.829
in about who was born
before that time?

00:26:01.829 --> 00:26:03.620
Raise your hands if
you're older than that.

00:26:03.620 --> 00:26:05.490
Thank you very much for
being in a room, all right?

00:26:05.490 --> 00:26:06.300
I appreciate that.

00:26:06.300 --> 00:26:08.900
You stay, all right?

00:26:08.900 --> 00:26:10.140
But here's my point.

00:26:10.140 --> 00:26:12.990
But within that period of the
'60s when people spoke truth

00:26:12.990 --> 00:26:15.530
to power, people of all
races spoke truth to power,

00:26:15.530 --> 00:26:17.660
we did see all kinds of changes.

00:26:17.660 --> 00:26:20.240
For example, we went through
the Civil Rights Act,

00:26:20.240 --> 00:26:22.810
and the Higher Education Act,
and the Voting Rights Act-- all

00:26:22.810 --> 00:26:24.827
were passed in that
period, legislation.

00:26:24.827 --> 00:26:26.160
And what difference did it make?

00:26:26.160 --> 00:26:30.890
Let me tell you, that even
here in Boston, in the '60s,

00:26:30.890 --> 00:26:34.270
we could not have had a
room looking like this.

00:26:34.270 --> 00:26:37.230
You could not have had a woman
chancellor of MIT, Cynthia,

00:26:37.230 --> 00:26:38.840
wherever she is--
you get my point?

00:26:38.840 --> 00:26:40.660
For women, for
people of color, it

00:26:40.660 --> 00:26:42.850
would not have been the case.

00:26:42.850 --> 00:26:45.640
And the world is different.

00:26:45.640 --> 00:26:47.870
When I ask Americans
the question,

00:26:47.870 --> 00:26:51.420
what percent of Americans do you
think had a college education

00:26:51.420 --> 00:26:53.420
when I went to jail in 1963?

00:26:53.420 --> 00:26:54.190
What do you think?

00:26:54.190 --> 00:26:54.950
Just spit it out.

00:26:54.950 --> 00:26:55.700
Somebody say it.

00:26:55.700 --> 00:26:56.450
What do you think?

00:26:56.450 --> 00:27:00.195
What percent of Americans
completed college?

00:27:00.195 --> 00:27:02.930
Ten percent.

00:27:02.930 --> 00:27:05.033
This would be MIT knowing
that, wouldn't you, huh?

00:27:05.033 --> 00:27:06.770
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:06.770 --> 00:27:07.610
Absolutely right.

00:27:07.610 --> 00:27:08.705
It was only 10%.

00:27:08.705 --> 00:27:11.798
What percent of whites?

00:27:11.798 --> 00:27:13.199
30.

00:27:13.199 --> 00:27:14.600
7.

00:27:14.600 --> 00:27:15.529
I'm hearing 30 and 7.

00:27:15.529 --> 00:27:17.320
And you all are supposed
to know some math.

00:27:17.320 --> 00:27:18.611
I have 10% for the whole group.

00:27:18.611 --> 00:27:20.510
The majority of folks
were white, right?

00:27:20.510 --> 00:27:22.290
Come on, math people
in the room, right?

00:27:22.290 --> 00:27:24.930
It was only 11%!

00:27:24.930 --> 00:27:27.750
And for blacks?

00:27:27.750 --> 00:27:29.050
Don't be embarrassed to say it.

00:27:29.050 --> 00:27:30.010
That's what Itoro told us.

00:27:30.010 --> 00:27:30.750
He said, say the truth.

00:27:30.750 --> 00:27:31.981
Say whatever it is, right?

00:27:31.981 --> 00:27:32.480
Say it.

00:27:32.480 --> 00:27:35.110
It was only 2% to 3%, right?

00:27:35.110 --> 00:27:38.287
And we were not even
counting other groups then!

00:27:38.287 --> 00:27:40.120
We were talking black
and white in the '60s.

00:27:40.120 --> 00:27:41.220
In fact, watch this.

00:27:41.220 --> 00:27:43.460
How many of you in this
room remember something

00:27:43.460 --> 00:27:44.870
called black and white TV?

00:27:44.870 --> 00:27:45.750
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:45.750 --> 00:27:46.960
The older people.

00:27:46.960 --> 00:27:50.187
And how many students here
have always had color TV?

00:27:50.187 --> 00:27:51.520
That's disgusting, it really is.

00:27:51.520 --> 00:27:53.190
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:53.190 --> 00:27:55.620
Today though, we go to
all the different groups

00:27:55.620 --> 00:27:56.256
in our society.

00:27:56.256 --> 00:27:58.130
So, what percent have
a college degree today?

00:28:01.312 --> 00:28:03.700
30.

00:28:03.700 --> 00:28:06.150
It's just about 30%, 31%.

00:28:06.150 --> 00:28:07.930
What percent of whites?

00:28:07.930 --> 00:28:09.790
[INAUDIBLE]

00:28:09.790 --> 00:28:11.640
It's up to about 37%.

00:28:11.640 --> 00:28:14.000
What percent of Latinos
and Hispanics, the fastest

00:28:14.000 --> 00:28:16.610
growing in our country?

00:28:16.610 --> 00:28:18.030
It's just at 15%.

00:28:18.030 --> 00:28:20.790
What percent of blacks?

00:28:20.790 --> 00:28:21.743
It's about 20%.

00:28:21.743 --> 00:28:22.701
What percent of Asians?

00:28:25.407 --> 00:28:27.692
It's at 55%.

00:28:27.692 --> 00:28:29.650
But there are certain
groups of Asian-Americans

00:28:29.650 --> 00:28:31.370
where that's not the case.

00:28:31.370 --> 00:28:33.680
It's very important when
talking about diversity,

00:28:33.680 --> 00:28:34.964
to go to specificity.

00:28:34.964 --> 00:28:36.880
There's a difference
between my black students

00:28:36.880 --> 00:28:38.338
whose parents are
from the Islands,

00:28:38.338 --> 00:28:40.780
and those whose parents
are from South Carolina,

00:28:40.780 --> 00:28:42.260
or those who are from Nigeria.

00:28:42.260 --> 00:28:45.226
There are differences in
the Hispanic population.

00:28:45.226 --> 00:28:47.600
My kids who are from Puerto
Rico, who are from New York--

00:28:47.600 --> 00:28:49.010
very different from
my kids who are

00:28:49.010 --> 00:28:50.360
from the University
of Puerto Rico.

00:28:50.360 --> 00:28:50.930
You get my point?

00:28:50.930 --> 00:28:53.360
There are different challenges,
depending on where you're from.

00:28:53.360 --> 00:28:55.110
But the point is, you
put it all together,

00:28:55.110 --> 00:28:58.340
and we can tell you
that literally over 2/3

00:28:58.340 --> 00:29:01.990
of Americans over 25 today
did not go to college,

00:29:01.990 --> 00:29:03.690
have not graduated from college.

00:29:03.690 --> 00:29:04.700
Why is that important?

00:29:04.700 --> 00:29:06.970
Well, all of my friends, white,
black, Hispanic, whatever,

00:29:06.970 --> 00:29:09.595
Asian, will say Native Americans
numbers are below everybody's.

00:29:09.595 --> 00:29:10.314
You know that.

00:29:10.314 --> 00:29:11.980
We've got to keep
working on that issue.

00:29:11.980 --> 00:29:13.040
But here's the point.

00:29:13.040 --> 00:29:15.770
All of my friends will say,
Freeman, that couldn't be true.

00:29:15.770 --> 00:29:17.887
Everybody I know
went to college.

00:29:17.887 --> 00:29:19.110
Duh.

00:29:19.110 --> 00:29:20.170
Do you get it?

00:29:20.170 --> 00:29:21.340
Duh.

00:29:21.340 --> 00:29:24.950
MIT professors know
MIT professors.

00:29:24.950 --> 00:29:28.470
Grad students know
grad students, right?

00:29:28.470 --> 00:29:31.260
Lawyers know lawyers,
doctors know doctors,

00:29:31.260 --> 00:29:33.510
and plumbers, who make more
money than any of you all,

00:29:33.510 --> 00:29:35.450
know plumbers, all right?

00:29:35.450 --> 00:29:39.860
The point is that literally,
we've gone from 10%, to 30%,

00:29:39.860 --> 00:29:43.020
and every group has gotten
better off as a result of that.

00:29:43.020 --> 00:29:44.540
But here is the challenge.

00:29:44.540 --> 00:29:46.980
The bottom quarter
of our country,

00:29:46.980 --> 00:29:49.740
of the population
with every race,

00:29:49.740 --> 00:29:54.290
the bottom quarter of the
students of every race

00:29:54.290 --> 00:29:57.630
have a probability of graduating
from a four-year institution

00:29:57.630 --> 00:30:00.730
that's under 10%.

00:30:00.730 --> 00:30:03.160
I don't care whether you're
white, or black, or Hispanic,

00:30:03.160 --> 00:30:03.660
or Asian.

00:30:03.660 --> 00:30:06.140
Whatever the group,
right now, until we

00:30:06.140 --> 00:30:08.160
do more in terms
of intervention,

00:30:08.160 --> 00:30:10.730
to help with both the academic
skills-- in many cases,

00:30:10.730 --> 00:30:13.360
the little children in Atlanta,
or in Baltimore, or here

00:30:13.360 --> 00:30:14.820
in Boston.

00:30:14.820 --> 00:30:16.970
In terms of the academic
skills on the one hand,

00:30:16.970 --> 00:30:19.130
and then the issues
involving financial aid.

00:30:19.130 --> 00:30:21.089
Because the financial
aid issue for poor people

00:30:21.089 --> 00:30:23.504
is not that they can't get it
from the federal government,

00:30:23.504 --> 00:30:25.360
it's that nobody's
helping them to fill out

00:30:25.360 --> 00:30:27.314
the forms before the deadline.

00:30:27.314 --> 00:30:29.480
If you don't fill out the
forms before the deadline,

00:30:29.480 --> 00:30:31.340
you don't get the money.

00:30:31.340 --> 00:30:33.200
And if the forms are
as complicated as they

00:30:33.200 --> 00:30:35.910
are, like the IRS-- right?

00:30:35.910 --> 00:30:38.200
So it's the financial issues,
it's the academic issue,

00:30:38.200 --> 00:30:41.360
but it's also having
the dream, the vision.

00:30:41.360 --> 00:30:43.970
And so here is what I
want you to think about.

00:30:43.970 --> 00:30:46.120
On the one hand,
as your students

00:30:46.120 --> 00:30:48.470
were saying, it is
so important that we

00:30:48.470 --> 00:30:52.030
look at our own institutions,
in higher education,

00:30:52.030 --> 00:30:55.050
to understand why students
of all backgrounds,

00:30:55.050 --> 00:30:57.230
black students and others,
Hispanics and others,

00:30:57.230 --> 00:30:59.110
are saying around
the country, there

00:30:59.110 --> 00:31:00.900
are challenges on
these campuses.

00:31:00.900 --> 00:31:04.540
We're not necessarily made
to feel like we belong here.

00:31:04.540 --> 00:31:07.130
Or people are making all kinds
of statements and assumptions.

00:31:07.130 --> 00:31:10.520
I'll never forget when I got
an A on the algebraic topology

00:31:10.520 --> 00:31:13.509
test in grad school, and I'm
the only little black kid

00:31:13.509 --> 00:31:14.050
in the class.

00:31:14.050 --> 00:31:15.591
And the professor
writes on my paper,

00:31:15.591 --> 00:31:17.935
you did surprisingly well.

00:31:17.935 --> 00:31:20.310
Now remember, I told you, my
mother's an English teacher.

00:31:20.310 --> 00:31:22.760
I go up and I say,
why the adverb?

00:31:22.760 --> 00:31:25.500
[LAUGHTER]

00:31:25.500 --> 00:31:27.770
He didn't know what
I was talking about.

00:31:27.770 --> 00:31:30.540
I liked that, I liked that.

00:31:30.540 --> 00:31:34.379
And when I pointed to the word,
surprisingly, all he could do

00:31:34.379 --> 00:31:34.920
was turn red.

00:31:34.920 --> 00:31:36.670
The advantage that
black people have is we

00:31:36.670 --> 00:31:38.336
don't turn red, all right?

00:31:38.336 --> 00:31:39.710
Got to put humor
into this stuff.

00:31:39.710 --> 00:31:41.668
Tell the truth, but put
a little humor into it.

00:31:41.668 --> 00:31:42.650
Ease it a little bit.

00:31:42.650 --> 00:31:47.240
And amazingly, just
recently, that person

00:31:47.240 --> 00:31:49.440
sent me the message
that in his own career,

00:31:49.440 --> 00:31:52.790
as he retired, that I had
taught him the best lesson

00:31:52.790 --> 00:31:55.350
about looking in the mirror.

00:31:55.350 --> 00:31:57.120
And the assumptions
we make, if you've not

00:31:57.120 --> 00:31:59.420
seen a little black kid
in a topology class, you

00:31:59.420 --> 00:32:00.600
assume it can't happen.

00:32:00.600 --> 00:32:03.750
When we said at UMBC
that we wanted students

00:32:03.750 --> 00:32:11.930
not only to succeed, but to be
excelling, to be the very best.

00:32:11.930 --> 00:32:14.480
And when we said, we want
to be able to place students

00:32:14.480 --> 00:32:17.986
on the faculties of the best
institutions in the country,

00:32:17.986 --> 00:32:19.610
that we wanted to
produce not just MDs,

00:32:19.610 --> 00:32:22.120
but MD PhDs, we
wanted people to dare

00:32:22.120 --> 00:32:26.340
to know, and to be at the
top, people said, impossible.

00:32:26.340 --> 00:32:27.700
Impossible.

00:32:27.700 --> 00:32:30.900
I'll never forget when I
went to one big foundation

00:32:30.900 --> 00:32:32.884
and talked about
evaluating the program,

00:32:32.884 --> 00:32:34.300
and I'll never
forget them saying,

00:32:34.300 --> 00:32:36.000
Freeman, it's going
to take generations

00:32:36.000 --> 00:32:38.469
before blacks will get PhDs.

00:32:38.469 --> 00:32:40.010
And the person looked
at me and said,

00:32:40.010 --> 00:32:42.220
you don't have the
gravitas yet in your race.

00:32:42.220 --> 00:32:44.560
He said that to me!

00:32:44.560 --> 00:32:45.910
And it was amazing!

00:32:45.910 --> 00:32:48.370
I looked at him and he said,
now you're an exception,

00:32:48.370 --> 00:32:49.880
but you don't have the gravitas.

00:32:49.880 --> 00:32:50.630
You get my point?

00:32:50.630 --> 00:32:54.290
People always want to make
the one person the exception.

00:32:54.290 --> 00:32:55.830
Yeah, I went through
school early.

00:32:55.830 --> 00:32:57.288
Yeah, I finished
my math major when

00:32:57.288 --> 00:33:01.334
I was 18, because I'd had
advantages, and dared to know.

00:33:01.334 --> 00:33:02.500
It wasn't about being smart.

00:33:02.500 --> 00:33:05.430
It was about being blessed to
have people who kept working

00:33:05.430 --> 00:33:06.840
on me and focusing on me.

00:33:06.840 --> 00:33:09.300
Anybody in here, whether
you come from money or not,

00:33:09.300 --> 00:33:10.740
and many of you
don't, but you had

00:33:10.740 --> 00:33:13.970
somebody in your home,
somebody in the school, who

00:33:13.970 --> 00:33:16.870
took interest in you,
and made the difference.

00:33:16.870 --> 00:33:21.690
You are the most privileged
human beings in the world.

00:33:21.690 --> 00:33:25.350
And at MIT, when I came
here years ago, Rafael,

00:33:25.350 --> 00:33:28.330
with my dear friend,
Mark Ryden, and talked

00:33:28.330 --> 00:33:30.380
20-some years ago
to all the chairs.

00:33:30.380 --> 00:33:32.370
And we were talking
and he was saying,

00:33:32.370 --> 00:33:34.476
these departments
represent the best

00:33:34.476 --> 00:33:35.600
of their kind in the world.

00:33:35.600 --> 00:33:38.110
And its inspiring.

00:33:38.110 --> 00:33:40.950
But here is what I want
you to think about.

00:33:40.950 --> 00:33:43.880
As you think about being the
most privileged-- all of you,

00:33:43.880 --> 00:33:46.520
students, faculty
and staff-- we must

00:33:46.520 --> 00:33:48.570
learn to look in the
mirror at ourselves.

00:33:48.570 --> 00:33:50.380
For the students,
I would say you

00:33:50.380 --> 00:33:52.880
should say what you're feeling,
what you're experiencing.

00:33:52.880 --> 00:33:56.050
At the same time, know that
this experience is preparing you

00:33:56.050 --> 00:33:56.890
for the next level.

00:33:56.890 --> 00:33:58.720
Because the more
advantaged you are,

00:33:58.720 --> 00:34:02.200
the more you'll continue to be
the only one wherever you go.

00:34:02.200 --> 00:34:04.310
I have always walked in
and been the only one.

00:34:04.310 --> 00:34:07.660
And people have always wondered,
is he really qualified.

00:34:07.660 --> 00:34:09.929
After 25 years as president!

00:34:09.929 --> 00:34:11.909
I still get that sometimes.

00:34:11.909 --> 00:34:13.830
It is what happens, right?

00:34:13.830 --> 00:34:15.449
Why am I telling you that.

00:34:15.449 --> 00:34:16.900
Yes, we want to tell the truth.

00:34:16.900 --> 00:34:19.719
But we need to decide,
who are we, as a student.

00:34:19.719 --> 00:34:23.219
Who am I, as a professor, as
a president of MIT or UNBC.

00:34:23.219 --> 00:34:25.020
And what difference can I make.

00:34:25.020 --> 00:34:27.940
I would say to you, as the
most privileged in the world,

00:34:27.940 --> 00:34:30.404
you have the capacity
to solve any problem.

00:34:30.404 --> 00:34:32.820
You're solving some of the
greatest science and technology

00:34:32.820 --> 00:34:34.429
problems that exist.

00:34:34.429 --> 00:34:37.699
On my own campus, we
are working on STEM.

00:34:37.699 --> 00:34:39.920
More than half my
students are in STEM.

00:34:39.920 --> 00:34:41.320
Students from all
over the world,

00:34:41.320 --> 00:34:43.957
and I see the intensity of
students from other countries.

00:34:43.957 --> 00:34:46.290
I use that to get my students
from this country that get

00:34:46.290 --> 00:34:48.520
that sense of that intensity.

00:34:48.520 --> 00:34:50.810
If you look at the
Nobel laureates

00:34:50.810 --> 00:34:53.170
throughout the 20th
century, so many of them

00:34:53.170 --> 00:34:54.754
came, at the time
in the 20th century,

00:34:54.754 --> 00:34:55.753
from European countries.

00:34:55.753 --> 00:34:57.980
Their parents didn't speak
English well in New York.

00:34:57.980 --> 00:35:00.438
They went to the poor-man's
Harvard, City College, Brooklyn

00:35:00.438 --> 00:35:03.054
College-- Why did they do
so well from the humanities

00:35:03.054 --> 00:35:03.720
to the sciences.

00:35:03.720 --> 00:35:07.220
That hunger, that intensity--

00:35:07.220 --> 00:35:08.955
We have a Chesapeake
Bay retriever

00:35:08.955 --> 00:35:11.080
at our campus-- a dog.

00:35:11.080 --> 00:35:13.110
And we have the statue of it.

00:35:13.110 --> 00:35:14.600
He's our mascot.

00:35:14.600 --> 00:35:16.840
And my basketball team
reads really well.

00:35:16.840 --> 00:35:17.970
I want to tell you that.

00:35:17.970 --> 00:35:19.553
Meaning they don't
win a lot of games.

00:35:19.553 --> 00:35:22.080
But they read really
well, all right,

00:35:22.080 --> 00:35:24.640
and we're great in debating
and great in chess--

00:35:24.640 --> 00:35:26.100
we do really well.

00:35:26.100 --> 00:35:28.280
Lacrosse does better,
soccer does well.

00:35:28.280 --> 00:35:31.050
But we are a nerdy
place, all right.

00:35:31.050 --> 00:35:32.811
And we've got 100
companies on campus

00:35:32.811 --> 00:35:34.310
and what we are
always talking about

00:35:34.310 --> 00:35:35.570
is, what makes the difference.

00:35:35.570 --> 00:35:37.920
These are companies started
by my students and faculty.

00:35:37.920 --> 00:35:39.230
What makes the difference.

00:35:39.230 --> 00:35:44.450
It is that we call
ourselves the House of Grit.

00:35:44.450 --> 00:35:50.050
The Chesapeake Bay retriever
is named a True Grit.

00:35:50.050 --> 00:35:51.600
And we're making a
point to America,

00:35:51.600 --> 00:35:53.320
stop talking about
this kid is smart

00:35:53.320 --> 00:35:55.460
and that kid is not smart.

00:35:55.460 --> 00:35:57.140
It's about how hard you work.

00:35:57.140 --> 00:35:58.990
It's about having
support from people

00:35:58.990 --> 00:36:00.530
to help you pull
you into the world.

00:36:00.530 --> 00:36:02.655
What makes the difference
in the Meyerhoff program,

00:36:02.655 --> 00:36:05.660
is that faculty on our campus--
most of whom are white,

00:36:05.660 --> 00:36:08.290
most of whom are still white
men-- advances got me up

00:36:08.290 --> 00:36:10.172
to 39% women
assistant professors,

00:36:10.172 --> 00:36:11.755
though so give us a
big hand for that.

00:36:11.755 --> 00:36:12.505
Big hand for that.

00:36:17.740 --> 00:36:20.750
And we're just getting in
our first people of color

00:36:20.750 --> 00:36:21.810
in these departments.

00:36:21.810 --> 00:36:24.470
But the key is, that
my white guys get it.

00:36:24.470 --> 00:36:26.260
If you look at my new
book, the publishers

00:36:26.260 --> 00:36:29.220
wanted to know why aren't you
putting blacks on the cover.

00:36:29.220 --> 00:36:33.360
I said no, because in science,
power is across races.

00:36:33.360 --> 00:36:35.970
And white guys are
powerful, am I right?

00:36:35.970 --> 00:36:37.520
My best friends, right.

00:36:37.520 --> 00:36:41.230
So Mike Summers, he was the
investigator, my dear friend,

00:36:41.230 --> 00:36:42.950
has produced more
African Americans

00:36:42.950 --> 00:36:44.783
who have gone on to get
PhDs in biochemistry

00:36:44.783 --> 00:36:46.020
than anybody in the country.

00:36:46.020 --> 00:36:48.670
And so he is there with
the HIV virus, there.

00:36:48.670 --> 00:36:51.950
People are thinking it's
HIV, because he studies that.

00:36:51.950 --> 00:36:54.280
And what's amazing is I've
got a young black man there,

00:36:54.280 --> 00:36:54.820
Meyerhoff.

00:36:54.820 --> 00:36:59.460
Then I've got one of my mentees
who's a young Korean American.

00:36:59.460 --> 00:37:03.520
All of them looking at
this disease and thinking,

00:37:03.520 --> 00:37:05.160
this is a problem of humankind.

00:37:05.160 --> 00:37:07.900
And therein is the dream.

00:37:07.900 --> 00:37:12.690
That people of all
races, men and women,

00:37:12.690 --> 00:37:14.500
come to know each
other well enough,

00:37:14.500 --> 00:37:16.310
trust each other
well enough, work

00:37:16.310 --> 00:37:18.520
hard enough, that
they work to solve

00:37:18.520 --> 00:37:21.180
the problems of humankind.

00:37:21.180 --> 00:37:23.090
Give the dream
round of applause.

00:37:23.090 --> 00:37:27.505
The dream of solving the
problems of humankind.

00:37:27.505 --> 00:37:29.090
[APPLAUSE]

00:37:29.090 --> 00:37:33.880
And so, I want you to listen
to Marketplace from yesterday.

00:37:33.880 --> 00:37:36.690
I was so pleased, it
just happened to come up.

00:37:36.690 --> 00:37:39.400
They've got interviews of
my faculty in the morning

00:37:39.400 --> 00:37:44.210
and the m and its on confronting
biases in faculty hiring.

00:37:44.210 --> 00:37:46.210
And the significance to
the approach we're using

00:37:46.210 --> 00:37:48.450
is while there are
people of color helping

00:37:48.450 --> 00:37:50.380
in the training,
the real ambassadors

00:37:50.380 --> 00:37:53.150
and trainers are all white.

00:37:53.150 --> 00:37:56.010
Whites are the most highly
respected on my campus.

00:37:56.010 --> 00:37:59.460
Who've agreed to come together
to work collaboratively

00:37:59.460 --> 00:38:00.350
with faculty.

00:38:00.350 --> 00:38:02.690
To help us to
diversify the faculty.

00:38:02.690 --> 00:38:07.472
Give my campus a hand for
having the courage to do that.

00:38:07.472 --> 00:38:09.370
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:09.370 --> 00:38:12.080
It's on NPR, from
Marketplace from yesterday.

00:38:12.080 --> 00:38:14.660
And so, I want you to
think about the students.

00:38:14.660 --> 00:38:16.770
I want you to think
about the notion,

00:38:16.770 --> 00:38:19.820
yes you want to think about
things on this campus.

00:38:19.820 --> 00:38:22.360
But you also want to think
about things in this community

00:38:22.360 --> 00:38:24.190
and back in your
own communities.

00:38:24.190 --> 00:38:26.060
Because I cannot say it enough.

00:38:26.060 --> 00:38:28.561
You are among the
most privileged.

00:38:28.561 --> 00:38:31.060
And you want to deal with the
issues you as much as you can.

00:38:31.060 --> 00:38:32.830
It takes time for
some things to happen.

00:38:32.830 --> 00:38:34.860
But you need to support.

00:38:34.860 --> 00:38:36.620
When you're having
a hard way, you

00:38:36.620 --> 00:38:39.200
need to know and believe
that a sense of self

00:38:39.200 --> 00:38:40.730
is most important.

00:38:40.730 --> 00:38:44.190
You must build a community
around yourself of people,

00:38:44.190 --> 00:38:47.180
and she said, of peers--
Itoro-- of peers,

00:38:47.180 --> 00:38:49.010
of people who consider
you, it'll be OK.

00:38:49.010 --> 00:38:51.160
And then you want to identify
faculty and staff who

00:38:51.160 --> 00:38:52.240
will give you that support.

00:38:52.240 --> 00:38:54.198
And you want to get into
those offices and say,

00:38:54.198 --> 00:38:55.520
I need your help.

00:38:55.520 --> 00:38:58.240
Never keep it to yourself.

00:38:58.240 --> 00:39:00.830
If you've got problems,
tell somebody.

00:39:00.830 --> 00:39:02.290
Say, I need this help.

00:39:02.290 --> 00:39:04.509
Whether it's in a class
or it's about emotional

00:39:04.509 --> 00:39:05.800
experiences that you're having.

00:39:05.800 --> 00:39:08.600
And ask questions in the lab.

00:39:08.600 --> 00:39:10.410
Ask questions in the classroom.

00:39:10.410 --> 00:39:13.060
Ask questions in your
dorm, wherever you are.

00:39:13.060 --> 00:39:15.360
It was I. Rabi,
the Nobel laureate,

00:39:15.360 --> 00:39:17.480
who said, when he was
growing up in New York,

00:39:17.480 --> 00:39:19.959
all of his friends'
parents-- mothers

00:39:19.959 --> 00:39:22.500
particularly-- would say, what
did you learn in school today.

00:39:22.500 --> 00:39:24.471
He said, but not
much Jewish mother.

00:39:24.471 --> 00:39:25.970
He said, my Jewish
mother would say,

00:39:25.970 --> 00:39:29.850
Izzy, did you ask a
good question today.

00:39:29.850 --> 00:39:32.310
And the practice of
encouraging his curiosity

00:39:32.310 --> 00:39:34.150
made him the thinker he became.

00:39:34.150 --> 00:39:38.460
So I challenge you MIT,
to ask the good questions.

00:39:38.460 --> 00:39:42.060
Not just about science
and technology,

00:39:42.060 --> 00:39:43.420
but about humankind.

00:39:43.420 --> 00:39:45.580
About the human condition.

00:39:45.580 --> 00:39:48.070
What will it take to have
more diverse faculty.

00:39:48.070 --> 00:39:49.580
If students are not
having the right

00:39:49.580 --> 00:39:52.140
experiences, are we
listening their voices,

00:39:52.140 --> 00:39:54.720
not just because they're
impressive as truth tellers,

00:39:54.720 --> 00:39:56.130
but because we
want to figure out

00:39:56.130 --> 00:39:59.604
the strategies with specificity
that could change environment.

00:39:59.604 --> 00:40:02.020
Because students have been
asking questions on this campus

00:40:02.020 --> 00:40:04.400
and all of our campuses
for at least 30, 40 years.

00:40:04.400 --> 00:40:06.920
It's been a 50 year
experiment that we

00:40:06.920 --> 00:40:10.980
have been working to pull
more people into this work.

00:40:10.980 --> 00:40:13.170
And my final point
to you involves

00:40:13.170 --> 00:40:16.020
the notion of struggle.

00:40:16.020 --> 00:40:20.260
Anybody who loves math, or
science, or reading TS Eliot

00:40:20.260 --> 00:40:23.570
understands that the most
fascinating work comes

00:40:23.570 --> 00:40:25.430
when you have to dive into it.

00:40:25.430 --> 00:40:30.400
I'm reading TS's The Wasteland
for 35 years to understand.

00:40:30.400 --> 00:40:33.640
Every time I read it,
layers of understanding

00:40:33.640 --> 00:40:35.310
coming from the
experiences I've had.

00:40:35.310 --> 00:40:38.810
So my wife and I just
celebrated a big anniversary.

00:40:38.810 --> 00:40:43.230
And we gave ourselves the gift
of going back to learn French.

00:40:43.230 --> 00:40:44.260
And so--

00:40:44.260 --> 00:40:45.990
[SPEAKING FRENCH]

00:41:00.670 --> 00:41:03.420
Give me a big hand,
45 years of marriage.

00:41:03.420 --> 00:41:05.550
45 years.

00:41:05.550 --> 00:41:08.650
But I leave you with one
statement and one quote

00:41:08.650 --> 00:41:09.800
from a poem.

00:41:09.800 --> 00:41:12.395
It was Apollinaire, the poet.

00:41:12.395 --> 00:41:16.530
And one of his poems that
says, "La joie venait tojours,

00:41:16.530 --> 00:41:21.890
apres la peine." "La joie
venait tojours, apres la peine."

00:41:21.890 --> 00:41:23.480
The joy comes
after the struggle.

00:41:23.480 --> 00:41:25.940
All of this is about a struggle.

00:41:25.940 --> 00:41:28.560
Anybody who's working to
learn anything substantive

00:41:28.560 --> 00:41:30.090
is struggling.

00:41:30.090 --> 00:41:32.730
Anybody's who's trying
to change at UNBC or MIT

00:41:32.730 --> 00:41:35.850
is struggling because it is
not hard, it is not easy.

00:41:35.850 --> 00:41:38.620
The Academy does not change
easily for any of us.

00:41:38.620 --> 00:41:40.450
It takes effort.

00:41:40.450 --> 00:41:42.980
1954, the schools
were desegregated.

00:41:42.980 --> 00:41:45.870
In the '60s, I still
couldn't go to a school

00:41:45.870 --> 00:41:46.930
that was integrated.

00:41:46.930 --> 00:41:48.150
You get my point?

00:41:48.150 --> 00:41:50.800
We have to keep working at it.

00:41:50.800 --> 00:41:53.520
You know, in that last year
before Janice Lumpkin died,

00:41:53.520 --> 00:41:56.810
when she died, I
asked her, what has

00:41:56.810 --> 00:42:01.710
helped you to be the
woman of courage you are?

00:42:01.710 --> 00:42:05.680
She was such a
powerful presence.

00:42:05.680 --> 00:42:09.040
And she said, I
remember how blessed

00:42:09.040 --> 00:42:13.020
I am to have had wonderful
parents, who gave me support.

00:42:13.020 --> 00:42:15.570
I remember both the
professors who supported me,

00:42:15.570 --> 00:42:18.010
and those who didn't
think I would make it.

00:42:18.010 --> 00:42:19.950
Both inspired me.

00:42:19.950 --> 00:42:25.710
She said, and I understood
that nothing takes the place

00:42:25.710 --> 00:42:28.230
of hard work and attitude.

00:42:28.230 --> 00:42:31.620
And I'm determined to help
my students understand

00:42:31.620 --> 00:42:35.700
that of those to whom much
is given, much is required.

00:42:35.700 --> 00:42:37.540
MIT, you do what we're doing.

00:42:37.540 --> 00:42:40.720
You are supposed to be placing
the students of all races, men

00:42:40.720 --> 00:42:43.370
and women, on the faculties
across this country.

00:42:43.370 --> 00:42:46.570
You're supposed to be the place
the says, I'm a MIT graduate.

00:42:46.570 --> 00:42:48.320
I believe.

00:42:48.320 --> 00:42:51.760
You can do this, you have
the best brains in the world,

00:42:51.760 --> 00:42:53.650
of every race here.

00:42:53.650 --> 00:42:56.770
I challenge you
MIT, be the best.

00:42:56.770 --> 00:42:59.020
Not just in STEM,
but for humankind.

00:42:59.020 --> 00:43:01.430
Watch your thoughts,
they become your words.

00:43:01.430 --> 00:43:03.470
Watch your words, they
become your actions.

00:43:03.470 --> 00:43:05.460
Watch your actions,
they become your habits.

00:43:05.460 --> 00:43:07.940
Watch your habits, they
become your character.

00:43:07.940 --> 00:43:10.400
Watch your character,
it becomes your destiny.

00:43:10.400 --> 00:43:11.610
Dreams.

00:43:11.610 --> 00:43:12.370
And values.

00:43:12.370 --> 00:43:14.840
Thank you all, very much.

00:43:14.840 --> 00:43:17.590
[APPLAUSE]

